message.content:
Today is October 2026. A researcher wants a NEW lossless speculative-decoding algorithm that raises draft ACCEPTANCE, especially at LONG draft lengths (8–16+ tokens) and at high concurrency. Their measurements (vLLM, Gemma 4 / Qwen 3.x targets, DFlash and native MTP drafters) show: (1) acceptance length does not change with concurrency (4 to 128 requests); (2) the conditional acceptance P(position j accepted | j-1 accepted) is roughly CONSTANT along the draft (~0.65–0.80 for DFlash, ~0.76–0.84 for MTP, with MTP's first position slightly higher), so long-draft acceptance decays geometrically and tau is capped near 1/(1-alpha). A long draft only pays at high concurrency if alpha >= ~0.88.

Task: search thoroughly (arXiv 2025–Oct 2026, OpenReview ICLR 2026/2027 submissions, ICML/NeurIPS/ACL/MLSys 2026, GitHub, vLLM/SGLang speculators, blogs) for work that specifically aims to RAISE ACCEPTANCE (per-position acceptance or acceptance length), especially for long drafts. Categories to cover:
- drafter training objectives that target acceptance length directly (acceptance-aware / expected-accepted-length losses, RL or policy-gradient for drafters, on-policy distillation over multi-step rollouts, training-time test, exposure-bias fixes) — e.g. EAGLE-3 TTT, HASS, GRIFFIN and any 2025–2026 successors (EAGLE-4? "learning to draft", "AcceptLoss", "LK loss", "DraftRL", etc.);
- architectures that keep later draft positions conditioned on the target (feature refresh mid-draft, re-anchoring, partial target passes, cross-attention to target KV, multi-layer target features), or that model dependencies between drafted tokens for long blocks;
- analyses of WHY acceptance decays with depth (error compounding vs inherently unpredictable tokens / entropy), per-position acceptance curves, and theoretical upper bounds on acceptance length;
- methods that increase accepted length beyond the first mismatch (block/sequence-level verification, "partial acceptance", "resampling continuation") — 2025–2026 ones;
- drafter scaling laws (does a bigger drafter or more training data raise per-position acceptance?), test-time adaptation / online drafter updates in 2025–2026;
- hybrid drafting that switches between model-based and retrieval drafting to get long runs (SuffixDecoding hybrid, etc.).

Return 12–25 of the most relevant papers (do not include ones already well known pre-2025 unless they are needed as anchors: EAGLE-3, HASS, GRIFFIN, Gumiho, Falcon, Hydra, ReDrafter, Online SD, DistillSpec — you may include them briefly as anchors). Follow the JSON schema in /tmp/lit2026/SCHEMA.md exactly (read it first) and write the array to /tmp/lit2026/D_acceptance.json. Verify every arXiv id on arxiv.org. Work efficiently; do not write any other files. In your returned summary, also state whether any paper already reports the "constant conditional acceptance with depth" observation, and what the strongest reported per-position acceptance / acceptance length at long drafts is (model, drafter, setting).

input.command:
cd /tmp && python3 -I -c "
import json; d=json.load(open('/tmp/lit2026/D_acceptance.json'))
print(type(d).__name__, len(d))
for e in d: print(e['arxiv'], e['first_version'], '|', e['short'], '|', e['venue'], '|', e['max_draft_len_evaluated'], e['max_batch_evaluated'])
"; ls -la /tmp/lit2026/

